<p>I think macOS keyboard shortcut philosophy is superior to Windows or Linux.</p>
<h2 id="i-dont-like-linux-shortcuts">I don&rsquo;t like Linux shortcuts</h2>
<p>They are very much the same as the Windows ones - which is funny considering the history between the two.</p>
<p>The shortcuts are absolutely disrespectful to my hands. I have to move my palms constantly to do things.</p>
<h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>
<h3 id="firefox-and-other-browsers-on-linux-vs-macos">Firefox (and other browsers) on Linux vs macOS</h3>
<p>On Linux, if I type my query into the URL bar, get some records from history or search, and want to select a particular one, what should I do?</p>
<p><em>Press Tab</em> — this one is alright, not too bad. This will select the next candidate in the suggested list. But what if I want to select the previous one?<br>
<em>Press Shift+Tab</em> — yeah, OK, I can&rsquo;t do it without moving my palms.<br>
<em>Can you just use the arrow keys?</em> — No, I cannot, because it requires moving my palms as well. It&rsquo;s unnecessary.</p>
<p>On macOS I can use readline style shortcuts with Control. I can press <strong>Ctrl+n</strong> or <strong>Ctrl+p</strong> to navigate down and up among the elements.</p>
<h3 id="any-input">Any input</h3>
<p>How do I control my cursor position on Linux when I type? I use the arrow keys. There is no other way. I can use some global remappers, but they break other things. They are alien to the system.</p>
<p>On macOS I can just press <strong>Ctrl+b</strong> to move backward or <strong>Ctrl+f</strong> to move forward. It&rsquo;s so much easier and more comfortable.</p>
<h2 id="its-just-a-matter-of-habit">It&rsquo;s just a matter of habit</h2>
<p>It isn&rsquo;t.<br>
Humans can get used to anything, sure, but there is no need to waste this adaptation resource. How is it possible that we accepted <strong>Ctrl+Shift+Tab</strong> as a key binding to switch tabs in our browsers on Linux?</p>
<p>Humans can&rsquo;t press it comfortably.</p>
<h2 id="i-wish-linux-would-utilize-the-win-key-more">I wish Linux would utilize the Win key more</h2>
<p>It would be such a great improvement. <strong>Ctrl+n</strong> in Firefox on Linux always spawns a new window.
It&rsquo;s so painful. Why not make it <strong>Win+n</strong> to spawn a new window?</p>
<p>Or when I type something in Thunderbird, wouldn&rsquo;t it be great to just press <strong>Ctrl+b</strong> to quickly go backward to fix a typo?<br>
Hands don&rsquo;t move - only the fingers.</p>
<h2 id="there-are-no-fixes-to-it">There are no fixes to it</h2>
<p>GNOME, for example, has Emacs mode, which doesn&rsquo;t work in some applications, including Firefox.</p>
<p>Firefox has the ability to remap the modifier key (from Control to Windows key), but it only works in Firefox and breaks a bunch of other things.</p>
<p>There are quite a few programs that can help solve this, but they aren&rsquo;t perfect (far from perfect, in fact). They require so much attention and configuration, and will break as soon as you install a new application that has its own shortcuts implemented in a weird way.</p>
<p>The only way would be to fix it at the fundamental level: maybe X11 / Wayland (although Wayland doesn&rsquo;t handle keypresses, does it?).</p>
<p>Maybe one day.</p>